why 3 3/4 DOUBLING The tune climbs to E while the tenor keeps its E; the doubled third keeps the held note.
why 5 0 OVERLAP The alto drops a fifth to D, below where the tenor was, while the tenor falls to A. They move down together, so the order stays clear.
why 5 0 SIMILAR_MOTION All voices fall into the D major chord (IV⁶) as the new phrase starts, each by its own interval.
why 6 0 PARALLEL_FIFTHS Soprano, tenor and bass all move from E–B to D–A, with passing C's in soprano and bass in between. Bach hears the passing notes as separating the chords; strict exercises still count these fifths.
why 6 0 PARALLEL_OCTAVES Tenor and bass go from E to D in octaves, with the bass's passing C in between. For Bach the passing note breaks them up; strict exercises still count them.
why 7 1/4 DOUBLING Soprano (C–B) and tenor (A–B) meet on B by step from opposite sides.
why 9 1/4 DOUBLING As in bar 7, soprano (C–B) and tenor (A–B) meet on B by step.
why 12 1/4 PARALLEL_OCTAVES Tenor and bass move D to C in octaves, with the bass's B in between. For Bach the bass's dip separates them; strict exercises still count it.
